  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+8}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, what is the value of $A\\cdot B?$",
  "worked_solution": "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+8 & =A(x-2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+8 & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ 10 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+8 & =A(0-2)+10 \\\\ -2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B=1\\cdot 10=10.\n$$",
